      \field{abstract}{It has been established that ecosystem functioning is affected by species richness, but the effect of inequalities in the relative abundances of these species — species unevenness — is less well studied. An investigation of the effect of initial species evenness in more than a thousand microbial microcosms, each containing 18 denitrifying species, now suggests that the more uneven an ecosystem is, the more sensitive it is to environmental stress. The stability of net ecosystem denitrification in the event of salinity stress was greater for communities that started life with high species evenness than for those in which there was extreme dominance by one or a few species.}
